WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.755
Changing the world isn't always a good thing,&nbsp;
even if it's with a really catchy song.

00:00:05.755 --> 00:00:10.680
A new character holds surprising depths in the new&nbsp;
"One Piece" movie – and her first song makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:15.360
her plans clearer than you think. Warning:&nbsp;
spoilers ahead for "One Piece Film: Red."&nbsp;

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:21.660
"One Piece Film: Red" is the 15th movie adapted&nbsp;
from a 25-year-old manga series, but its success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:26.880
shows this franchise is anything but tired.
"Film: Red" broke records in Japanese cinemas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:32.040
netting more than 15 billion yen in just over&nbsp;
its first month of availability and becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.640
one of the most profitable releases&nbsp;
to ever run in Japanese theaters.&nbsp;

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:40.200
Uniquely for the series, "Film: Red" is something&nbsp;
of a musical. While its characters don't express&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:44.880
themselves in song in traditional musical&nbsp;
theater fashion, the movie revolves around a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:48.609
new character named Uta who performs songs&nbsp;
that underscore the events of the movie.&nbsp;

00:00:48.609 --> 00:00:53.520
In total, "Film: Red" features seven songs&nbsp;
available to fans on a soundtrack EP that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:58.320
effectively doubles as an album by Ado, the&nbsp;
real-life singer who performs Uta's vocals.&nbsp;

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.542
While Uta initially appears to be an&nbsp;
altogether happy and optimistic pop star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.542 --> 00:01:06.780
some heartbreaking events in "Film: Red"&nbsp;
eventually reveal her true goal. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:11.640
her purpose may be hidden from viewers at first,&nbsp;
looking back on the lyrics to the first song Uta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.259
performs in the movie hints at Uta's true nature.

00:01:14.259 --> 00:01:15.990
"Why are you so chummy with Princess Uta?!" 

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:17.732
"Yeah, tell us!"

00:01:17.732 --> 00:01:22.020
Early on in "One Piece Film: Red," viewers&nbsp;
learn that Uta became a popular singer after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.500
broadcasting recordings of her music&nbsp;
all over the world in an attempt to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:29.160
ease the lives of those negatively&nbsp;
impacted by the New Age of Piracy.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:33.720
Partway into the film, however, viewers find&nbsp;
out that Uta's singing traps its listeners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:38.640
into a dreamworld over which she has full&nbsp;
control. Furthermore, in a dark twist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.260
she harbors intentions to end her own life,&nbsp;
taking everyone trapped in her dreamworld&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:48.840
along with her. Her widely-broadcast concert and&nbsp;
massive popularity leads to her having influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:53.340
over a significant portion of the world's&nbsp;
population, who she wants to help permanently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:57.930
escape from suffering under piracy. They're&nbsp;
decent intentions – but it's not a good plan.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:01:59.820
The first song Uta performs in "Film:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:04.560
Red" is titled "New Genesis." While the song at&nbsp;
first seems to be about creating better world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.660
some lyrics betray a darker meaning – like
"Say goodbye to everyone who stands against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.500
us / No one has the power to stop&nbsp;
a change that comes through music."&nbsp;

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.480
and
"Say goodbye to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:20.400
everything that's weighing you down / Transcend&nbsp;
beyond reality to colorful worlds of music."&nbsp;

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:25.136
The lyrics may sound innocuous enough on the&nbsp;
first listen, but with the benefit of hindsight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.136 --> 00:02:31.440
Uta's real plan starts to become perfectly clear.
Of course, Uta's motivations are also entirely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.940
understandable. She sees other people suffering,&nbsp;
and intends to use her power to stop it.&nbsp;

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:40.800
When she sings lyrics like "We can choose&nbsp;
the way of our future / Try a brand new path,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:45.840
if I change the world," she's not necessarily&nbsp;
masking some sort of hidden evil, but simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.800
indicating that she wants to change the&nbsp;
world, albeit in a misguided manner.&nbsp;

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:55.140
At the film's climax, Luffy naturally stops Uta&nbsp;
and saves the people trapped in her dreamworld.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:59.520
Which isn't surprising – throughout all&nbsp;
of "One Piece," Luffy also wants to change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:03.729
the world for the better, but does so by&nbsp;
confronting and defeating evil head-on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.801 --> 00:03:09.660
Uta, then, serves as Luffy's foil in "Film: Red,"&nbsp;
sharing an interest in positive change but acting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:14.880
on the goal in a more antagonistic fashion.
"Film: Red" producer Shinji Shimizu highlighted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:18.180
Uta's uniqueness in an interview&nbsp;
with Anime News Network. He said,&nbsp;

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:22.414
"In 'One Piece' films of the past,&nbsp;
there's always very strong, bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.414 --> 00:03:27.060
dare I say stereotypical types of enemy figures&nbsp;
that Luffy and his gang would beat up and fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:31.380
but with this movie, that is different."
Considering Uta's appearance as a pop idol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:36.240
as opposed to a fearsome warrior, he's certainly&nbsp;
right about that. But regardless of her looks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:39.840
Uta's intentions to change the&nbsp;
world, regardless of consequences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:44.162
are clear – and if you pay attention to the&nbsp;
lyrics, it's all there right from the start.

